# Development standards

## Code changes
- Match existing conventions in the file you edit (naming, imports, patterns).
- Self-explanatory code over comments; comment only non-obvious business logic.
- Run the project's linter/tests for touched areas before marking done.

## Git workflow
- Feature branches off main; descriptive commits; PRs with clear what/why.
- Never force-push shared branches without explicit approval.
- Keep PRs focused — split large changes when possible.

## Testing
- Add tests only when they cover real behavior (not trivial assertions).
- State success criteria in prompts: command to run, URL to check, expected output.

## Security
- No secrets in code, rules, or chat. Use env vars and `.env.example`.
- Validate user input at boundaries. Review auth and data-access changes carefully.

## Documentation
- README: what, how to run, how to test — under 150 lines for AI context.
- Architecture decisions → `docs/adr/` or wiki pages, not inline rule bloat.
- See `readme-optimize` skill and `.cursor/reference/readme-structure.md`.
